	(text "Zeners are 15V/1W"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 232.156 170.18 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify right)
		)
		(uuid "033508bb-bae8-4835-8011-701c92d259e4")
	)
	(text "R20/R21 must have adequate pulse-energy/inrush capability.\nEach rail charges 10,000 µF at startup (~15.7 J stored per rail at 56 V).\nUse pulse-rated wirewound/chassis resistors and/or\ncurrent-limited/soft-start supply."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 327.152 223.52 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "4372f615-217f-4540-ae6e-857a54ea16a6")
	)
	(text "LF VOICING:\n~55 Hz input HPF + ~57-60 Hz passive output-coupling pole are cascaded,\nso total electrical LF rolloff starts higher than either pole alone.\nExact response varies somewhat with NFB setting.\nDespite this, LF damping falls strongly with frequency because of the\nseries output capacitor. Speaker/cab resonances therefore have more\ninfluence, so perceived bass can remain strong and \"bloomin'\" while\nunwanted sub-bass is reduced."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 224.282 95.504 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "8a8e526f-a350-4b15-9f85-8e1f33495082")
	)
	(text "Aiming for 280u-300u total capacitance.\nInto 8 ohms load it’s calculated\nclose to 60Hz cut-off (not only\nbass reduction but actually making\nlow end progressively looser,\nso damping factor drops more the\nlower it goes). Parallel caps are\nbetter in order to distribute current\nhandling demand, and not depend\ntoo much on the high current ripple\nratings for the caps."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 248.92 147.066 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "91e0ac2f-c88d-4e83-9b42-42e96c8abd4f")
	)
	(text "Biasing:\n\nStart with bias trimmer R8 set to minimum resistance.\nPower up with no load first: transformer disconnected,\ninput shorted to ground, and current limiting if available.\n\nMeasure DC millivolts across R1 and R2.\nBecause R1 and R2 are 1Ω, 1mV = 1mA.\n\nAim for 50mV-80mV across each resistor.\nLet the amp warm up for 10-15 minutes.\nMeasure again and adjust if needed.\nStart with minimum, 50mV. Then after\n10-15 of warming up adjust further to the\ntarget value of your choise, somewhere\nbetween 50mV and 80mV.\nIf minimum R8 already gives excessive idle current,\npower down immediately; do not continue adjusting.\nR1/R2 currents should settle reasonably close to\neach other. Large mismatch may indicate device/wiring problems.\n\nNegative feedback (NFB pot):\n\nCounter-clockwise = LOOSE,\nmaximum feedback resistance,\n(slightly above 1M, R18 + R19 + NFB pot)\nleast negative feedback,\nlowest damping.\n\nClockwise = TIGHT,\nminimum feedback resistance\n(44k, the sum of R18 and R19),\nmaximum negative feedback,\nhighest damping.\n\nNominal midband input impedance is around 33k.\nYou need a preamp/buffer in front of the amp\nthat can supply a line-level low impedance singnal.\n\nPut R4 and R5 as close to the gate pins as possible.\nAlso put the zener diodes D2, D3, D4, and D5 as close\nto the MOSFET pins as possible.\n\nAttach Qbias to the same heatsink as Q-Nch and Q-Pch.\nPut it between them; it needs thermal tracking.\nQvas does not need to be attached to the heatsink.\n\nMake sure all transistors attached to the heatsink are\nelectrically isolated from the heatsink with mica or\nsilicone pads and insulated screw bushings.\nVerify isolation with a multimeter before power-up.\nMake sure no transistor legs are shorted to the\nheatsink.\nThere should be no continuity between the heatsink\nand any leg of any transistor attached to it.\n\nR1/R2/R3/R20/R21 should be attached to\na properly sized separate heatsink.\nR1/R2/R3/R20/R21 should not deliberately heat the\nQbias/MOSFET thermal-tracking heatsink if you can avoid it.\nQbias is supposed to respond to MOSFET temperature;\nheating it with supply/output resistors can make the\nbias compensation react to the wrong heat source.\n\nI strongly recommend to add a coolign fan for the\ntransistors heatsink (Q-Nch, Q-Pch, and Qbias)."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 18.542 18.542 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "c35d6e80-6ad9-472a-8fee-86ed4ed7ce95")
	)
	(text "Make sure R25 pulse rating is sufficient\nto handle the surge current at startup,\nwhen the caps are charging."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 53.086 169.672 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "cbcbe17c-80aa-4228-87fa-8b7c49290024")
	)
	(text "Input inverting stage (to make the amp\nin-phase relative to the input). And also\ntone shaping (high-pass set to around\n55Hz at input)."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 87.122 181.356 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "d3eaf011-3ccb-4acd-89b6-e0f6cdd38980")
	)
	(text "Make sure that the transformer secondary\npolarity is in-phase with the primary.\nThe first signal test should be made with the\nNFB pot at maximum resistance (minimum pot position).\nThen reduce resistance gradually (turn the pot clockwise).\nIf gain increases, the amp becomes unstable, or it howls\nas feedback is increased, the feedback-secondary polarity\nis wrong and the two feedback wires must be reversed.\nWith more NFB there should be less gain, not more."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 337.566 141.224 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "e992eaf6-4fda-4f13-b78f-5d66c869b691")
	)
